+---
+name: coordinator-cycle
+description: Use when running this template repo as the coordinator — settling a row's scope before it is claimed, cutting a lane, dispatching the builder, the spine author and the cross-family reviewer, adjudicating in the lane, and landing. It is the one per-lane procedure from filing to landing, so a session follows this file instead of a chain of handoffs.
+stacks: [any]
+domains: [any]
+phases: [dev, gate]
+tags: [coordinator, lane, review, adjudication, landing, workflow]
+scope: this-repo
+---
+
+# Coordinator cycle (this template repo)
+
+You are the **coordinator**. You claim rows, cut lanes, dispatch the builder,
+the spine author and the reviewer, verify and commit their work, adjudicate
+through the entry point, and land. This file is the **procedure**; the
+current handoff is the **state** (what landed, what is open, what the owner
+owes). Exact commands, landing steps and environment traps are in
+[references/recipes.md](references/recipes.md). Read it before your first
+claim and when a step below points there.
+
+Read beside it:
+- `session-protocol`: the commit bar (§3), the records (§4), the coordinator
+  close-out (§4) and the commit style (§5).
+- `spine-authoring`: "In a lane — one change set, authored and judged whole".
+
+**When a session learns a correction, it edits this skill or its recipes in the
+close-out commit, not the handoff.** A correction kept only in a handoff
+reaches the next session only if that session follows the chain back. That
+chain grew to twelve files before this skill replaced it.
+
+## Roles
+
+| Role | Who | Session |
+|---|---|---|
+| Build | the `kit-builder` agent (Claude Opus, medium) | one per round; leaves its change uncommitted |
+| Spine author | GPT Terra (`gpt-5.6-terra`, medium) | **one retained session per lane**, resumed each turn |
+| Code review | Codex 6.1 Sol (`gpt-6.1-sol`, high) | **fresh per review**; it must not remember what it approved |
+| Adjudication | a retained Claude Opus home, via `coordinator_adjudicate.py` from the lane | the entry point retains it; never a subagent |
+
+No role judges what it authored: Terra authors and Opus judges, Opus builds
+and Sol reviews. The adjudicator's ruling on a dispute is final.
+
+## 1. Before the claim: settle scope and trust where the row is born
+
+Scope is decided when a row comes into being: filed by hand, or minted from a
+lane as a successor, a disposition or a split. It is never decided by a guard
+on a row that already exists. Before such a row can be claimed, one fresh Sol
+session runs the kit's `prompts/critique.template.md` over its spec and
+answers two more questions:
+
+- **Name each independently landable deliverable, and which lands first.** If
+  the owner keeps several together on purpose, record that in the spec so no
+  later session argues it again.
+- **Does any deliverable give a file authority over a hold, an act or a gate?**
+  If yes, the spec gains a `## Trust` section before the claim:
+  - **Producer:** who writes the file, by which route, and what a failed write
+    looks like (failed call, timeout, structured error, partial file).
+  - **Consumers:** every reader, found by grep and listed by path.
+  - **Ruling:** what each consumer does with an absent, unreadable, failed or
+    malformed input, and what the authority binds to.
+
+  The owner confirms or overrules the Ruling like any decision. PROCESS.md §3
+  "When a guard is owed" is the rule; this section applies it.
+
+  Why: WI-841's verdict files gained authority without a stated boundary, and
+  eight fresh reviews found eight routes around it. The ruling that closed
+  them (an act's authority comes from the judgement of its own rows) was a
+  design decision made at gate 7.
+
+The spec also names the change set's **anchor**, the approved row the spine
+change hangs from (`spine-authoring`, "In a lane").
+
+## 2. Claim
+
+Take the coordinator lease, then claim the whole batch under one scoped
+unpause: a reviewed deletion of `docs/work/pause`, the claims, and a
+byte-identical restore. The steps and the traps are in recipes §2.
+
+## 3. The lane: intent, iterate, checkpoint
+
+The lane runs in three phases. Code and spine are not worked in alternation:
+the code settles first, then the spine is written and judged as one set.
+Alternating them made every code fix re-open its rows. At WI-841 that meant
+19 sittings, with two LLRs re-attested about eight times each.
+
+### Intent: once, at the start
+
+1. The builder builds the first round. You verify, regenerate and commit.
+2. Terra authors the **whole change set in one turn**, as `Drafted` rows, from
+   the anchor down: every new and amended row, plus the arms map
+   (`spine-authoring`, "In a lane"). There is no sitting yet. The rows give the
+   reviewers the obligations the code is meant to meet.
+
+### Iterate: code only
+
+Each round:
+
+1. **Confirm the commit landed** (`git log -1` moved) before any review. A
+   hook-refused commit makes Sol review an empty range.
+2. **A narrow Sol review** of the round's delta. The brief asks Sol two things
+   besides the findings, which are the kit reviewer template's two clauses:
+   - name the failure classes this change admits, and hunt those first;
+   - for each fix that adds a check, say why the defect cannot be made
+     unrepresentable instead.
+   Tell Sol that the rows are stale by design until the checkpoint, so it
+   judges the code only.
+3. **The sweep table, before any fix.** The builder's first output for a
+   review is a table with one line per finding:
+
+   | Finding | Class | Every site of the class (with the grep that found them) | The one owning boundary that ends the class |
+   |---|---|---|---|
+
+   Check the table before dispatching the fix: a class with one site and no
+   grep is a table that has not been swept. The table goes into the next
+   review's brief, and that review tries one site the table does not list.
+   Why: WI-841 fixed the carrier check for TOML, then CSV, then markdown, then
+   a conversion: four rounds for one class.
+4. **Findings are claims.** The builder confirms or refutes each one on the
+   real code path before changing anything. It acts only on verified evidence
+   that no setting or OS retry mitigates.
+5. **A consolidation outside the spec's named surface stops the round.** When
+   the fix needs one owner for something several modules outside the spec
+   already read, the builder reports it rather than building it. You file a
+   preparatory row on trunk, land it as its own lane, and rebase this lane onto
+   it. The §1 Consumers list usually predicts this before the build starts.
+6. **Trace fields only.** The builder keeps `Implements:`, the LLR module and
+   symbols, and the TC evidence current on each commit, so
+   `check_trajectory --strict` stays green. It appends every row change it
+   would make to the lane's spine change list. Terra and the adjudicator do not
+   run in this phase.
+7. **A dispute goes to the adjudicator.** A third round of crafted-input
+   findings is a named dispute, not another blind build round.
+
+### Checkpoint: when a narrow round has no BLOCKER or MAJOR
+
+1. **Terra, resumed, reconciles the whole set** against the settled code: the
+   accumulated spine change list plus the arms map.
+2. **The closure check passes** before the sitting (`spine-authoring`, "In a
+   lane": parents, the arms, TC-to-LLR-and-SR, interface citations, the strict
+   integrity checks).
+3. **One combined sitting** (`--brief combined`) judges every amendment, first
+   approval and Done-when change together, top-down. Returns go back by kind:
+   - spine text goes to Terra;
+   - code goes to the builder;
+   - a byte-exact one-assertion fix you may apply yourself, openly and
+     recorded.
+   A follow-up answered in the lane moves out of `## Dispositions` to another
+   heading. Re-sit only what came back.
+4. **The fresh, full-lane Sol gate**, from the lane's trunk base to its tip,
+   reviews code and rows together. If it finds code defects, go back to
+   iterate, and hold one more checkpoint before landing. Expect two or three
+   checkpoints per lane.
+
+Before the final gate, restore the trunk-only generated views on the lane
+(recipes §3). The module-size stamp is the exception: the lane carries it.
+
+## 4. Land
+
+1. Rebase onto trunk. Never merge-refresh a lane that has committed spine rows.
+2. Take the act as the lane's last commit: Status flips and the snapshot only,
+   with the verdicts committed first.
+3. Squash, regenerate, close the row, archive the tips, sweep, and close the
+   re-mint citing the act.
+
+Lanes that take acts land one at a time. Every step and its trap is in
+recipes §3.
+
+## 5. Decisions and the owner
+
+Record every call made on the owner's behalf in
+`docs/decisions/<branch>.toml` (`coordinator-<date>.toml` for calls on trunk),
+with the riskiest in `high_risk`. Ask the owner to **confirm or overrule**,
+never to approve. A decision only the owner can make, and that blocks a row,
+becomes a pending open item cited by the row's `needs`; then move to the next
+row. Stop for an owner signature on a new need or a frame external crossing.
+
+## 6. Close-out
+
+Follow `session-protocol` §4, "Coordinator close-out". Add each correction this
+session learned to this skill or its recipes (see the top of this file). The
+handoff links the one it replaces and holds state only: what landed, what is
+open, what the owner owes, and the session prompt.
+
+## Never
+
+- Push or merge to main. Push is the owner's.
+- Rule an open item, or sign a held rung or any act reserved to the owner.
+- Leave `docs/work/pause` deleted.
+- Read `OWNER_SCRATCHPAD.md`, or the adjudicator's token file. Keep only the
+  token file's path.
+- Self-review, or adjudicate through a subagent.
+- Skip a hook, or add a fallback, degenerate or legacy path. Fix the single
+  point of failure instead.
+- Change `~/.claude` settings, or install software the handoff does not name.
